The pin is not ours to choose. `crates/kernels-cuda/csrc/CMakeLists.txt`
fetches the same tree for the ahead-of-time build:

    CPMAddPackage(
      NAME flashinfer
      GITHUB_REPOSITORY flashinfer-ai/flashinfer
      GIT_TAG v0.6.15
      GIT_SHALLOW TRUE
      DOWNLOAD_ONLY YES)

and the carried files were copied from the tree that command produced --
`.../build/_deps/flashinfer-src/include/flashinfer/` -- rather than from a
fresh clone, so that the text carried here is byte-for-byte the text the AOT
build compiles. Seventeen copies of that tree exist under `target/`, one per
build hash; all seventeen were compared and are identical.

### What is carried

Twenty-four files: the transitive `#include` closure, inside the FlashInfer
tree, of the headers `csrc/src/attn/` names --
`attention/{cascade,decode,mask,mla,prefill,state,variants,
default_decode_params,default_prefill_params}.cuh` and
`{fastdiv,layout,page,pos_enc,utils}.cuh`. 13,662 lines, 589,556 bytes.

It was twenty-eight, and the four that left are the subject of "What was
deleted" below. `attention/scheduler.cuh` used to be one of the seeds of this
closure, because the ahead-of-time crate's `.cu` files included it; under the
JIT nothing includes it and it is gone. The line count was 16,084 until the
host C++ inside the twenty-four came out as well -- same section, second
half.

Not carried, and deliberately: `attention/hopper/*`, `comm/*`,
`gemm/bmm_fp8.cuh`, `cutlass_utils.cuh` and `mamba/selective_state_update.cuh`.
They are outside the closure of those seeds, they are a different
architecture's problem (sm_90 TMA, NVSHMEM, CUTLASS), and carrying text
nobody compiles would be weight in every binary.

### What was deleted

Four files, and this is the one change in this tree that is not a guard.
`attention/scheduler.cuh`, `exception.h`, `allocator.h` and
`attention/heap.h` contained no device code at all -- no `__global__` body, no
`__device__` function, 2,138 lines and 97,847 bytes of host C++ between them.
Every one was already guarded in its ENTIRETY, so NVRTC had always seen four
empty files; the guards were doing a deletion's work at the cost of shipping
the text, and `csrc/` is device text only.

Each had a Rust owner before it was deleted, which is why this is a deletion
and not a port: `src/plan/{decode,prefill,mla,sm90}.rs` for the scheduler,
`src/plan/error.rs`, `src/plan/alloc.rs` and `src/plan/heap.rs` for the other
three, 3,853 lines of Rust that were written against these files and had been
carrying the behaviour for some time.

The measurement that made it safe: `scheduler.cuh` was `#include`d by nothing
in this tree, and `allocator.h` and `heap.h` only by `scheduler.cuh`. Walking
the four compilation roots reached 40 of the 43 internalised files and missed
exactly those three. `exception.h` was the fourth and the only reachable one
-- `page.cuh` and `utils.cuh` included it -- so deleting it deleted two
`#include` lines as well. That is safe for a reason worth stating plainly:
because `exception.h` was guarded in its entirety, `FLASHINFER_CHECK` and
`FLASHINFER_ERROR` were ALREADY undefined in every NVRTC translation unit
here, and the expansions that survive are all inside `#define` bodies NVRTC
never expands or host launchers it never reaches. What NVRTC sees did not
change by one token. (Removing the launchers, below, took the second kind
with them: 13 expansions remain and every one is a `#define` continuation
line.)

### And then the host C++ inside the surviving files went too

Four whole files was the easy half. The rule is *`csrc/` holds pure kernels*,
and after those four deletions it held in effect and not in letter: 34 guarded
regions carrying 1,503 lines of upstream host text were still on disk, hidden
from NVRTC by `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` and by XQA's own `#ifndef
GENERATE_CUBIN`, plus **1,055 lines in 13 `cudaError_t` host launchers that
were not guarded at all** -- `SinglePrefillWithKVCacheDispatched`,
`BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheDispatched` and their siblings across
`cascade.cuh`, `decode.cuh`, `mla.cuh` and `prefill.cuh`. Those thirteen
survived only by being uninstantiated templates, which is a far weaker shield
than a guard: a single explicit instantiation anywhere would have handed
`cudaMalloc`, `std::vector` and `cudaStreamCreate` to a device compiler. They
form a closed set -- the only callers of any of the thirteen are the others --
so removing them removed no reachable code.

A fifth whole file went with them: **`xqa/hostUtils.h`**, one `inline
cudaLaunchConfig_t makeLaunchConfig(...)`, no device text, included only from
the `mha.cuh` host block being deleted. It was found by the deletion rather
than before it.

Net across both trees: **15 files touched, 2,694 lines and 123,642 bytes
removed**, each site keeping a `// PIE: REMOVED` marker naming what stood
there -- 40 markers, and they are inside that net. `MODIFICATIONS` carries the
per-file ledger and the reasoning.

What was NOT removed: **12 two-sided regions, 36 host lines**, where the
`#ifndef` has an `#else` arm holding our device replacement -- `<cstdint>`
against XQA's `mha_stdheaders.cuh` shims, chiefly. The documented strip keeps
the guarded body and discards the `#else`, so deleting the host arm of one of
those would make a strip silently promote OUR text into a file that claims to
be upstream's. They stay, and they are the reason this section says *removed*
rather than *all removed*.

Deleting text a marker names is the one edit a strip cannot undo, and there
are now twelve files' worth of it rather than two `#include` lines.
`MODIFICATIONS` states it as step 3 of its transform and says plainly that
step 3 needs upstream's tarball.

### What was changed

Guards, markers, and the `#include` lines named above. **39 `// PIE:` markers
across 12 of the 24 files, 156 added lines**; the other 12 files are
byte-identical to upstream. Only **two** `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` directives
remain in the whole FlashInfer tree, both in `attention/mla.cuh`, both
two-sided; there were thirty-three markers over ten files when every guard was
still a guard.
Every copyright header is intact and untouched. Nothing was reformatted,
renamed, reordered or "improved" -- include order in particular is upstream's,
which is why several files carry four small markers where one large one would
have needed a line moved.

A guard existed because NVRTC compiles for the device only. It refuses a
function marked `__host__` outright ("a function explicitly marked as a
__host__ function is not allowed in JIT mode"), it has no C++ standard
library, and it has no include path -- so `<iostream>`, `<sstream>`,
`<vector>`, the `cudaLaunchKernel` wrappers and the `std::ostringstream` error
paths had to be hidden from it. They are no longer hidden: they are gone, and
a marker records each one. The ahead-of-time build that used to see upstream
exactly by defining no `__CUDACC_RTC__` no longer exists -- there is no nvcc
in this crate -- so nothing was left to serve by keeping the text.

Each change is preceded by a marker of one of two fixed forms:

    // PIE: guarded for NVRTC -- <what and why>. Upstream is unmodified apart
    // from markers of this form; see NOTICE. Removing them restores the file.

    // PIE: REMOVED -- <what stood here>. <N> lines of host C++, <why removing
    // it changes no compile>. This marker is one a strip does NOT undo; see
    // MODIFICATIONS.

One `grep -rn "// PIE:" csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/` finds all 39, of which 40
across both trees are `REMOVED`. The boilerplate sentence appears once per
file, on its first marker.
`csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/MODIFICATIONS` lists the count per file, and the
property that list asserts -- *strip the markers, the `#ifndef` under each and
its matching `#endif`, and put back what a REMOVED marker names, and every
file is byte-for-byte upstream* -- was checked mechanically, per file, against
the CPM tree before this NOTICE was written.
`tests/upstream_manifest.rs` re-checks the half of that claim which needs no
network: every file's guard count, added-line count, line count and byte count
still match the table, and the strip is still well defined for every file. The
other half -- that the stripped text equals upstream's -- needs the CPM
checkout and is a manual step on rebase.

Two consequences are worth recording because they are not visible in a diff:

  * `attention/scheduler.cuh` used to be guarded in its entirety, all 1,808
    lines of it, and that whole-file guard is what identified it as a
    deletion. It was the host planner -- `std::vector` bin-packing, workspace
    carving, `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor` -- and the three
    `__global__` declarations at its top were forward declarations that
    existed only to give the occupancy query a function pointer. There was no
    device code in it to keep. Under the JIT the plan is Rust's job, and the
    file is now gone rather than guarded; see "What was deleted" above.
  * `attention/default_prefill_params.cuh` loses its six `__host__`
    constructors, and `page.cuh` and `fastdiv.cuh` theirs, for the reason
    above: NVRTC rejects the *declaration*, not just the body. The structs and
    their `__host__ __device__` accessors -- which is what a kernel reads out
    of `__grid_constant__` -- are untouched, and the fields are filled from
    Rust.

### What is NOT FlashInfer's, and no longer sits next to it

The six small headers PIE wrote to stand in for names NVRTC does not ship --

    cstdint, type_traits, cuda.h, cuda_runtime.h, bit,
    boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp

-- used to share a directory with the FlashInfer text, which made this a
paragraph about disambiguation. They are now in `csrc/shim/`, a directory that
holds nothing else and whose README states the rule, so the disambiguation is
structural and this paragraph is only history. There are twenty-one of them
today; `csrc/shim/README.md` is the register, and it records each addition and
retirement rather than restating a total.

They are named after the headers they stand in for because an `#include`
resolves against a header set by exact spelling -- this crate has no include
path on disk, so the spelling in the directive *is* the identity of the
header. Each one exists because guarding its include was tried and measured to
break device code, and each says so in its own banner. In particular
`boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp` contains no Boost code and is not derived from
Boost: it is a one-line constexpr absolute value under a name FlashInfer asks
for.

### Where it lives, and why it moved

`csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/`, subtree shape exactly as upstream ships it, plus
`LICENSE` and `MODIFICATIONS` at its root. It was `csrc/vendor/flashinfer/`.

The move is the point, not a side effect: this crate compiles every kernel
through NVRTC, so `csrc/` is device text and nothing else, and a directory
called `vendor` said the opposite -- that the text was a guest with its own
rules. It is not a guest. It is compiled by the same JIT, guarded by the same
markers, and tested by the same tests as everything beside it.

**Not one byte of the twenty-nine changed in the move** -- all 28 source files
as the tree then stood, and `LICENSE`, zero changed lines each; only
`MODIFICATIONS`, which is PIE's
own file, was edited, to record the new path. The subtree shape is what makes
that true: upstream's files include each other relatively (`"../cp_async.cuh"`,
`"mask.cuh"`) and a relative spelling survives a move that carries the whole
tree. Four of those 28 were deleted afterwards and for an unrelated reason;
that is "What was deleted" above, not part of the move.

Seventeen `#include` directives changed across the whole internalisation, and
the split is worth stating because only one half of it touches upstream text:
**twelve in PIE's own files** -- ten `<flashinfer/attention/…>` and two
`<xqa/…>`, which is where an angled, tree-rooted spelling lived -- and **five
inside the XQA files themselves**, which had already been made tree-relative
for an unrelated reason (see the XQA section below) and simply gained the new
prefix. No FlashInfer file contains a changed directive.


