crate does not build; the rest are host code -- a `.cpp` and a `.cu` wrapper
whose job (tensor-map construction, kernel launch) is Rust's here, in
`driver-cuda/src/fire/xqa.rs`.

### What was changed

**Four of the fourteen files differ from upstream at all; the other ten are
byte-identical.** Those four are `mha.cuh`, `mha_components.cuh`,
`mhaUtils.cuh` and `utils.h`.

  * `mha.cu` is carried as **`mha.cuh`**. It is a rename and nothing else.
    This crate holds no translation units -- a `.cu` is an ahead-of-time TU
    for nvcc and there is no nvcc here -- and the rename is free because
    nothing includes it by its upstream name. Every `mha.cu:N` citation in
    this repo is against upstream; `mha.cuh:N` means our copy. The file's own
    header note gives the offset both ways and it is arithmetic, not memory:
    3,093 - 3,051 = 42.
  * The host tail of `mha.cuh` is **deleted**, not guarded. It used to sit
    under one `#ifndef GENERATE_CUBIN` of ours -- `configureKernel`,
    `launchMHAFlashInfer`, and with them `cudaMemcpyFromSymbol`,
    `cudaFuncSetAttribute` and `cudaLaunchKernelEx`. Upstream guards its *own*
    host launcher `launchMHA` exactly this way at `mha.cu:2820`; that tail was
    FlashInfer's downstream addition and they never guarded it. Both are gone
    now, along with upstream's `throw std::runtime_error("not implemented")`
    under `#if USE_INPUT_KV`. The replacement is `driver-cuda/src/fire/xqa.rs`
    and always was.
  * `utils.h` loses `checkCuda` and `checkCu`, two host error checkers that
    `throw std::runtime_error`. `utils.h` is included by `utils.cuh` and by
    `mha.h`, so it is genuinely inside the include closure -- but the four
    call sites were all in the `mha.cuh` host tail above, so after that tail
    went, nothing called either. They were dead text in a file NVRTC parses,
    which is the same shape as the four FlashInfer headers.
  * `xqa/hostUtils.h` is **deleted entirely**: one `inline
    cudaLaunchConfig_t makeLaunchConfig(...)`, no device text at all, and its
    only includer was the deleted `mha.cuh` tail.
  * Five `#include` spellings made tree-relative -- `"mma.cuh"` and
    `"utils.cuh"` become `"attn/xqa/mma.cuh"` and `"attn/xqa/utils.cuh"` in
    `mha.cuh`, `mhaUtils.cuh` and `mha_components.cuh`. This is the one change
    forced by carrying two upstream trees at once: FlashInfer *also* has an
    `mma.cuh` and a `utils.cuh`, NVRTC has a single flat include namespace
    keyed on the literal directive string, and a bare `"utils.cuh"` from XQA
    would otherwise resolve to FlashInfer's. Those five are the only bare
    spellings either tree shares; the other nine XQA directives name files
    FlashInfer does not have and were left alone.
  * 39 `// PIE:` marker lines across those four files -- 28 in `mha.cuh`,
    6 in `mha_components.cuh`, 3 in `mhaUtils.cuh`, 2 in `utils.h` -- in the
    same fixed forms used on the FlashInfer side. `grep -rn "// PIE:"
    csrc/src/attn/xqa/` finds all 39. It was 48 before the strip, and it went
    DOWN rather than up: fourteen of the old markers were *inside* the host
    tail and were deleted with it, and three new `REMOVED` markers replaced
    them. Markers went up on the FlashInfer side, 33 to 39, and down here, for
    the same operation. That is the reason to count them rather than reason
    about them.

Note the shape of the difference from the FlashInfer side of this document:
there, the changes began as 33 `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` guards; here there
were **none**. XQA was written to be compiled as a cubin -- it carries
upstream's own `GENERATE_CUBIN` switch for exactly that, and
`src/families/attn.rs` passes `-DGENERATE_CUBIN=1` -- so the JIT needed one
guard of ours where FlashInfer's host-facing headers needed thirty-three.
Both counts are now zero on the XQA side and two on the FlashInfer side, and
what stood behind them is deleted rather than hidden. Diffing XQA against a
fresh checkout is correspondingly cheap on ten of fourteen files and needs
`MODIFICATIONS` for the other four.


